---
import DocsPage from "../../components/DocsPage.astro";
import { loadReference } from "../../lib/batch3";

const page = loadReference();
const description = "The ChartKit v1 JSON spec: required alt text, chart types, and the validate and repair loop.";
const repair = `import { repair, validate } from "@chartkitdev/core/validate";
import { render } from "@chartkitdev/core/all";

const result = validate(spec);
if (!result.ok) {
  const fixed = repair(spec);
  if (fixed.ok) render(fixed.spec);
}`;
---

<DocsPage title="Spec reference" description={description} href="/docs/spec">
  <p>
    The schema is <a href="/schema/v1.json">chartkit.dev/schema/v1.json</a>. <code>validate()</code> checks the schema and the data. <code>render()</code> does not validate. Unknown types return an error SVG and do not throw.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Client behavior</strong>
    Tooltip, zoom, pan, brush, and animation duration run in <code>@chartkitdev/core/interact</code>. The tooltip defaults on for line, bar, area, scatter, combo, and donut. <code>a11y.keyboard</code>, <code>a11y.dataTable</code>, and <code>legend.interactive</code> stay off unless set, because turning them on loads extra interaction code. The static SVG records those choices in <code>data-ck-intent</code>. <code>tooltip.fields</code> chooses the rows and their order. A string is a field name. An object sets <code>title</code> and a <code>format</code> preset. <code>tooltip.position</code> is <code>auto</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>left</code>, or <code>right</code>. <code>tooltip.crosshairLabel</code> prints the hovered x value on the guide. <code>animation.easing</code>, <code>interaction.select</code>, and <code>theme.tokens.fontFamily</code> warn CK3012. Axis titles, tick counts, rotation, side, reversed and un-niced scales, padding, min height, and <code>a11y.description</code> are drawn.
  </div>
  <h2>Repair loop</h2>
  <figure class="code"><figcaption>ts</figcaption><pre><code>{repair}</code></pre></figure>
  <p>
    <code>repair</code> applies the safe JSON Patch on each error and returns <code>{`{ spec, ok, errors, applied }`}</code>. The input is not mutated. It does not invent data. A missing or unknown <code>type</code> is reported first.
  </p>
  <h2>Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>version</code> is <code>"1"</code>. <code>a11y.alt</code> is one sentence and is required.</li>
    <li><code>data</code> is an array of rows. It is not <code>{`{ "values": [...] }`}</code>. Large data may use <code>{`{ "columns": { "field": [] } }`}</code>.</li>
    <li><code>type</code> is the chart. <code>mark</code> is options: curve, sampling, stack, layout. Not a string.</li>
    <li>Gauge, kpi, and progress-ring use <code>metric</code>: value, min, max, delta, label. <code>mark.rings</code> stacks activity rings. <code>encoding.volume</code> draws a pane under candles. <code>metric.thresholds</code> colours a kpi from the value.</li>
    <li>Donut uses <code>encoding.x</code> for the slice label and <code>encoding.y</code> for the value.</li>
    <li>A second axis is <code>encoding.*.axis: "y2"</code>. Titles go in <code>axes.y2.title</code>.</li>
    <li>Field types are quantitative, temporal, ordinal, or nominal.</li>
    <li>A y range is <code>encoding.y.scale.domain</code>: <code>[min, max]</code>. Either end may be <code>null</code> to stay automatic, as in <code>[0, null]</code>. A y2 channel uses the same domain for the combo's second axis. Ticks stay inside that range, and points outside it are clipped to the plot.</li>
    <li><code>mark.sampling</code> defaults to <code>auto</code> and downsamples line-like charts above twice the plot width. <code>none</code> draws every point and is much slower at 100k and 1M.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Chart types</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="opt-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>What it draws</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          page.types.map((item) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={`/docs/charts/${item.type}`}>{item.type}</a></td>
              <td>{item.summary}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Field-level pages for the statistical and planning types also live at <a href="/spec">/spec</a>. <a href="/docs/spec-anatomy">Spec anatomy</a> is the short version of the required keys.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
